# Lock-Free SPSC Ring Buffer in C++

## Conceptual Overview

A **single-producer single-consumer (SPSC) ring buffer** is a fixed-capacity circular buffer where exactly one thread produces items and exactly one thread consumes them. Because there is only one writer to each index (the producer writes `head`, the consumer writes `tail`), we can avoid locks entirely by using atomic operations with carefully chosen memory orders.

The key insight: the producer and consumer never write to the same atomic variable. The producer only modifies `head_`, and the consumer only modifies `tail_`. This eliminates the need for compare-and-swap loops or mutexes.

## Data Structure

```
Buffer of capacity N (must be a power of two for efficient masking)

Indices:
  head_ : next position to write (owned by producer)
  tail_ : next position to read  (owned by consumer)

Layout:
  [0] [1] [2] ... [N-1]
   ^               ^
  tail_           head_

Full when: (head_ - tail_) == N
Empty when: head_ == tail_
```

We use **unsigned arithmetic** so that wrap-around is handled naturally by modular arithmetic. By using a power-of-two capacity, we can replace `% capacity` with `& (capacity - 1)`.

## Full Implementation

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <new>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <type_traits>

template <typename T, std::size_t Capacity>
class SPSCRingBuffer {
    static_assert((Capacity & (Capacity - 1)) == 0,
                  "Capacity must be a power of two");
    static_assert(Capacity > 0, "Capacity must be positive");

public:
    SPSCRingBuffer() : head_(0), tail_(0) {}

    // Non-copyable, non-movable
    SPSCRingBuffer(const SPSCRingBuffer&) = delete;
    SPSCRingBuffer& operator=(const SPSCRingBuffer&) = delete;
    SPSCRingBuffer(SPSCRingBuffer&&) = delete;
    SPSCRingBuffer& operator=(SPSCRingBuffer&&) = delete;

