# Lock-Free Single-Producer Single-Consumer Ring Buffer in C++

## Conceptual Overview

A **single-producer single-consumer (SPSC) ring buffer** is a fixed-size circular buffer where exactly one thread writes (produces) and exactly one thread reads (consumes). Because there is only one writer and one reader, we can avoid locks entirely by using atomic operations with carefully chosen memory orders.

The key insight: since there's only one producer and one consumer, there is no contention on the same index from multiple threads. The producer only modifies `head` (write index), and the consumer only modifies `tail` (read index). They never write to the same atomic variable, so we don't need compare-and-swap loops. We only need to ensure proper visibility of data written before an index update and proper visibility of index updates before data is read.

## Data Structure

```
+------------------+
|   buffer[0]      |
|   buffer[1]      |
|   ...            |
|   buffer[N-1]    |
+------------------+
        ^
        |
   head (producer writes here)
   tail (consumer reads here)
```

- `head`: next position to write (owned by producer)
- `tail`: next position to read (owned by consumer)
- Buffer is full when `(head + 1) % capacity == tail`
- Buffer is empty when `head == tail`

We reserve one slot to distinguish full from empty (standard technique).

